            "padding_top_desktop": "desktop:pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_desktop": "desktop:pb-0",
            "max_width_desktop": "95ch",
            "richtext": "<h4>When should a humidifier be used?<br/><br/></h4><h6>For relief:<br/></h6><ul><li>Chronic runny nose</li><li>Hay fever symptoms</li><li>Nose bleeds</li><li>Asthma and allergy flare-ups</li><li>Sinus congestion</li><li>Dry throat</li></ul><p></p><h6>For every day:</h6><ul><li>To create a balance home environment </li><li>As an overall preventative measure</li><li>During particularly hot or dry seasons</li></ul><h4><br/>Common concerns with humidifiers</h4><p>Despite the many benefits humidification can provide, humidifiers can create concern. Mold or bacteria can grow within dirty humidifiers, creating problems for those with asthma or allergies. Similarly, when humidity levels get too high, especially with mist releasing from the product, homeowners can start to experience moisture problems. These include signs that include mold, mildew, condensation on windows, and stains on the walls and ceiling.</p>",
            "richtext_style": "p2"
          }
        },
